5 #edb/ref 17592186552078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552382 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552382 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552382 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552382 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dbcb8564-443f-5a87-ab9d-12627df755da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d554be3-9b5d-5bb1-8354-199346e2e327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552437 #edb/ref 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =cos\\,(5\\pi (t+5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t+25\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡25\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡25\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+5)\\} & =L\\{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "669dd3eb-164c-527e-a5e3-447099f637bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552445 #edb/ref 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(2(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c235044-2675-5c70-a69d-3ac1bca6c5ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552452 #edb/ref 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+6t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =(t+4)^{2}+6(t+4)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+8t+16\\,+\\,6t+24\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+14t+42\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+14L\\{t\\}+42L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+42\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552457 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17